Z||000003||BobC||11-02-1999||09:54 PM||||It worked for me.  $300 sounds like pipe dreams, though...<P> Subject: A New V-8 Fiero Book!!!!!<BR> From: S. Joe Wynman<BR>                   <BR>Several months ago I told everyone that I was in the process of writing a book on how to install a longitudinally mounted V-8 into the Fiero.  Well<BR>it’s finally done!  I think this is the first book written on the Fiero since 1988.  <P>This book, entitled “GUIDELINES FOR INSTALLING A LONGITUDINALLY MOUNTED V-8 ENGINE INTO A FIERO” tells you how to install a Chevy,<BR>or just about any other GM V-8 into the Fiero.  Besides the engine and transmission, your total cost will be about $300! The transmission, axles,<BR>etc., are from the Oldsmobile Toronado (and that series of GM cars) and can be readily obtained for about $200. With these parts, you will have a<BR>reliable Drivetrain, which can stand up to the abuse of a V-8.<P>The book was written as a system concept.  Simply installing a V-8 into the Fiero will give you more power, but not necessarily a safe and reliable<BR>vehicle.  The other subsystems in the Fiero must be upgraded in order to deal with the increase in both weight and power.<P>Even if you never have any intentions of installing a V-8 into you Fiero, there is lots of other information dealing with suspensions, brakes, etc.<BR>upgrades of the Fiero.  If you want a 3800 series motor, the wiring and other information will simplify the installation.  <P>Newer engine constantly interfaces with the transmission.  If your intended conversion does not support this interface, the engine will operate in a<BR>reduced power mode.  For example, your Northstar conversion (not covered in this book) may only put out 140 HP if the transmission interface is<BR>not handled correctly.  This book covers the interface.  The same goes for the PassKey System and speedometer interface.<P>We are also working with a local fabricator in creating a full up kit.  If the negotiations can be worked out, we will be selling a V-8 kit for about<BR>$350.  Details will follow.<P>Most of the pictures are in color. <P>Catches<P>There are a few catches to the book.  These include:<P>Document Lock.  When you receive the CD-ROM, you will not be able to read or open it.  A password is required.  Each CD-ROM has it’s own unique<BR>password.  You will have to call Associated Auto, Inc., to obtain the password.  <P>No Printing: The book itself is not printable.  It is a feature utilized in the encryption of the book.  Since it is not practical to have your PC next to<BR>your Fiero while it is being worked on, several of the pages (such as the blue prints) are printable. Printed hard copies may be available in the<BR>future, depending on the results of the survey, but they would probably be pricey.<BR> <BR>Price: The book will be available at the introductory price of $15.00, which includes second day express mail.  After the introduction, the book price<BR>will be $24.95, plus $3.00 for shipping.  Payment must be made to:<P>Associated Auto, Inc.<BR>500 South River Street, Suite 110<BR>Hackensack, NJ 07601<P>Only checks and Money Orders can be accepted as payment.  MO’s will result in immediate shipping; checks require 10 days to clear.<P>Survey: Each Book comes with a survey.  The survey asks for your opinion on the book, things you feel are missing, unclear, too much details,<BR>typos, etc.  With the software format of the book, your inputs can be made immediately.  Also included in the package is a stamped, addressed<BR>envelope allowing you to easily return of the survey.<P>System Requirements: Just about anything out there.  On the lower end:<P>PC<BR>486, 33 MHz<BR>4 Meg RAM<BR>20 Meg HD Space<P>MAC<BR>Just about anything<BR>4 Meg RAM<BR>20 Meg HD Space<P>Stats:<P>Pages: 96<BR>Pictures: 180<BR>Wiring diagrams: 3 pages<BR>Wiring Tables: 2 <BR>Blue Prints: 3 pages<BR>Format: CD-ROM, PDF Encoded<BR>Regular Selling Price: $24.95 plus $3.00 for S/H.<BR>Introductory Selling Price, until 30 November 1999: $15.00, which includes S/H<P>Chapters from the Book:<P>Some Words from the Author, Preface, Acknowledgments, Dedications, Copy Rights, Disclaimer, Pictures Found in this Book, Welding, General Safety<BR>Instructions, Keep Your Work Area Clean, Don’t Force Any Component, Read All Notes and Cautions, Use the Right Tools, Use Safety Goggles, Don’t<BR>Overreach, Work in a Well Ventilated Area, Technical Support, Personal Recommendations, Media Format, Introduction, Axle Sizing, Axles for the<BR>1988 Fiero, A Brief History on the Transmission to be Used, Identifying the Transmission and Differential, Mix and Match, Advantages/Disadvantages<BR>of the Concept, Axle Sizing for the Project, Removing the Stock Motor, System Concept, V-6 Upgrade, Parts Sources, Transmission, Costs, Frame<BR>Theory, Wheel Sizing, Engine Transmission Selection, Adapter Plate Usage, Transmission and Differential Selections, Engine Height, Drive Axles Used,<BR>Suspension Upgrades, Replacing the Rear Coil Springs, Sway Bar, Installing the Sway Bar, Frame Mounts, The Problem, A Demonstration, Now for<BR>the Parts, Lower Control Arm Bushing Replacement, Transmission Selection, Torque Converter, Governor Springs/Vehicle Speed Sensor, Gear<BR>Selector Lever, Starter Mounting Modifications, Shifter Cable and Transmission Mount, Transmission Cooling, TVV Cable, Engine Modifications,<BR>Dowel, Oil Filter Relocation, Motor Mounts, Joining the Engine and Transmission, The Oil Pan, Oil Pan Modifications, Oil Pan Modification Procedures,<BR>Attaching the New Drivetrain to the Modified Fiero Cradle, 1984 Through 1987 V-8 to Fiero Cradle Mounting, 1988 V-8 to Fiero Cradle Mounting,<BR>Cradle Removal, Bump Steer Correction, Building a 1984 Through 1987 Fiero Bump Steer Correction Kit, Link Assemblies, Cradle Mounting Plates,<BR>Inner Pivot Assembly, Installing the Components, The Center Axle, Installing the Drive Train, Restoring the Fiero Trunk, Relocating the Battery, Air<BR>Cleaner Assembly, Fuel Pump, Fuel Pump Replacement, Pulsator, Routing the Fuel Lines, Cooling the V-8, Radiator Cap, Bleeding the Cooling System,<BR>Additional Cooling Methods, Cooling the Transmission, The Exhaust System, Headers, Exhaust Design, Accessory Mounting, Tips on Building the<BR>Adapter Harness, Components Used For the Adapter Harness, Main Power Junction, Interconnection Guidelines, Wiring Placements, The 1984 Model<BR>Year, The 1985 Through 1988 Model Years, Automatic Transmission Interface, Grounds, Speedometer Interface, ECM Location, Gages, Tachometer,<BR>Temperature Gage, Oil Pressure Gage, Volt Meter, Pass Key System, Installing the Throttle Cable, A/C Installation, Brakes, Emission Certification and<BR>DOT Approvals, Before You Start Your V-8 Conversion, Parts Sources, Photo Album <P>Enjoy!<P>||||reg||6
